From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0237] clock: gate a branch on one token and prove it running

C2-03 asks the backend to do the first write of the staged plan: take the PDM
XO/4 branch, the clock the debug counter already measures, and turn it on
through the public entry point instead of only reading it.

The card's shape is deliberate. The gate is its own consumer
(sweetclock-gate) over its own ledger token, so its demand is accounted for
separately from the measurement's reference; the verdict is the hardware
halt poll, not the value that was stored; the four neighbours the register
shares a block with are read back before and after the store and a moved
neighbour fails the card; and the branch is left enabled until the reboot,
with the token still held, because C2-04 owns the disable path and C2-05 owns
sharing. A refusal (already enabled, halt cleared, bad entitlement) leaves
the ledger untouched and reports through start() as an incomplete gate.

The seventh descriptor, localID 7 gcc_xo_div4_clk, exists so that identity
says what the register is: the C2-08 measurement reference. LocalID 4 stays
the PDM XO/4 branch and is what the gate drives. The measurement now also
samples the gated branch, which is the evidence the card wants - a counter
reading that only exists because this patch turned the clock on.

Guarded by a new opt-in property, sweet,clock-gate (u32, <= 1), which is
impossible to set from the shipped boot arguments; with it absent nothing is
stored, which is what the 0130 default image proves. The property comes from
the device-tree generator, so this patch carries it along with the two host
tests: the diff is the whole feature, replayable on its own.

    git apply -p1 xnu/patches/0237-mh2lm-clock-branch-gate.patch

